message.content:
Now I see the bug clearly. In `bcf_dec_typed_int1_safe`:
```c
if (end - p < bcf_type_shift[t]) return -1;
```
For INT32 (t=3), `bcf_type_shift[3] = 2`. So it checks `end - p < 2`. But `le_to_i32(p)` reads 4 bytes. So if `end - p` is 2 or 3, it passes the check but reads 4 bytes → heap-buffer-overflow.

Now, the issue with my PoC is that the shared data parsing is failing before reaching the INFO loop. The warnings show ID type 0 and FILTER type 7. This means the shared data is being read from the wrong offset.

Let me reconsider. The shared data I wrote is `07 17 41 00 13 00 00`. But the parsing shows ID type 0. This means the shared data being parsed is different.

Wait, let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that the header text parsing consumed the record bytes. Let me check by dumping the shared data. Let me modify the debug fuzzer to print shared data even on failure.

Actually, let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that `bcf_hdr_read` reads the header text, but the header text parsing in `bcf_hdr_parse` reads beyond the header text into the record.

Wait, actually, let me reconsider. The header text is 60 bytes. `bcf_hdr_read` reads `hlen` bytes (60) into `htxt`. Then `bcf_hdr_parse(h, htxt)` parses it. The parsing should stop at the null terminator.

But wait, maybe the issue is that the header text parsing in `bcf_hdr_parse` reads the sample line and then continues. Let me check. The `bcf_hdr_parse` loop:
```c
do {
    while (NULL != (hrec = bcf_hdr_parse_line(hdr, p, &len))) {
        ...
        p += len;
    }
    ...
    if ( strncmp("#CHROM\tPOS",p,10) != 0 ) {
        ...
    } else {
        done = 1; // Sample line found
    }
} while (!done);
```

So it parses lines until it finds the `#CHROM` sample line. Then `bcf_hdr_parse_sample_line(hdr, p)` parses the sample line. This should be fine.

Hmm, let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that the record isn't being read correctly. Let me dump the shared data. Let me modify the debug fuzzer.

Actually, let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that the header text parsing consumed the record bytes because the header text doesn't end with a proper newline structure. Let me check.

Actually, let me just dump the shared data. Let me modify the debug fuzzer to print shared data even when bcf_read fails.

